  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nAnother hot day is on tap for today as an H590 ridge continues to\nprevail over the area. Highs will be in the mid 90s to around 100\nacross the area with heat indices ranging from 100-105. Expect\nmostly dry conditions, but there could be a stray shower in the\nsoutheast. /15/\n\nMonday through next weekend...\n\nThe anomalous ridge responsible for recent record breaking heat \nbegins to break down early next week with the approach of a \nshortwave trough. There is a subtle moisture return in response to \nthis, which will increase dewpoints back into the low 70s. This \nshould both increase dangerous heat as well as rain chances \nbeginning Monday and persisting through Tuesday. Heat indices will \nrange from 100-105+, with the hottest temperatures west of I-55. An \nelevated heat graphic continues to be advertised. Would not rule \nout a heat advisory for Monday and possibly Tuesday. Further east,\nwhere low (20-30%) rain chances are focused, indices are likely \nto remain below criteria (105), but there will still be a limited \nheat threat. Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms, in \nperhaps the first sign of the cool season, will not be strictly \ndiurnal, and should continue overnight ahead of a slow moving \nfrontal boundary. Since synoptic forcing is still weak, this \nfrontal boundary will slowly sag south over the course of \nWednesday and into Thursday as the shortwave rounds the midlevel \nhigh. There is some difference among the guidance regarding the \ntiming of the main jet energy associated with the shortwave, but \nthere is good agreement, and it makes meteorological sense, that \nshortwave ridging will be reinforced in its wake late next week \ninto the weekend. Dry conditions will return with a continuation \nof above normal temperatures. /SAS/",
